That (the rings, I mean) explains the long semi-flamewar in the other
thread, then :-)

I finally had to take a quick look at the source code.  I see nothing
obviously wrong with the PRNG, although it's heavily conditional.

- They use modulo N to get a number in [0..N). That's unfair if N is
  large, but that's hardly the case here.

- Modulo N is also bad if the PRNG is predictable in the lower-order
  bits, but I don't think we have access to such PRNGs (not on Linux,
  anyway).

- Nethack ships with an old implementation of BSD random(), but at
  least as I build it it's not used.  And anyway I expect random()
  to have been a decent PRNG from the beginning, unlike the original
  Unix rand().

